# Python Conventions

[This is an example rules file. Replace the content with conventions observed in your actual codebase. Run Prompt 2 from the starter pack to auto-generate rules from your code.]

## Stack
- Python 3.11+
- pytest for testing
- ruff for linting and formatting
- pyproject.toml for dependency management

## Directory Structure (IDesign Layered)

```
src/
├── contracts/           # Protocols (interfaces), types, DTOs
├── engine/              # Pure business logic (no I/O)
├── data_access/         # Database adapters, API clients
├── utility/             # Cross-cutting helpers
├── infrastructure/      # DI, composition roots, provisional mocks
└── tests/
    ├── unit/            # Pure Python tests (no runtime deps)
    ├── component/       # Isolated tests with mocks
    └── integration/     # Cross-component tests
```

## Import Rules

| Layer | May import from |
|-------|----------------|
| `contracts/` | Nothing (leaf — only stdlib/third-party) |
| `engine/` | `contracts/` only |
| `utility/` | `contracts/` only |
| `data_access/` | `contracts/`, `utility/` |
| `infrastructure/` | `contracts/` |
| `tests/` | Any layer (test code is exempt) |

## Do
- Use `Protocol` (from `typing`) for interfaces, not ABCs
- Use `@dataclass(frozen=True)` for DTOs
- Use `pyproject.toml` optional dependency groups for test deps
- Keep engine functions pure: receive data, return results, no side effects
- Use pytest markers to gate tests by dependency: `@pytest.mark.pure_python`, `@pytest.mark.integration`

## Don't
- Use `Any` type annotation — use proper types
- Put business logic in `data_access/`
- Import from `engine/` or `data_access/` inside `contracts/`
- Use `requirements.txt` — use `pyproject.toml`
- Use mutable default arguments
